X_L = 2 \pi f L = 2 \times 3.14 \times 50 \times 4 = 1256 \Omega, so X_L / R = 1256 / 8 = 157 → (157)

24936:
\text{LiAlH}_4 is a very strong reducing agent that drives carboxylic acids all the way to primary alcohols \text{R} - \text{CH}_2\text{OH}
It cannot be stopped at the aldehyde stage, so the equation \text{R} - \text{COOH} + \text{LiAlH}_4 \rightarrow \text{R} - \text{CHO} is wrong
Rosenmund reduction, Stephen reduction and PCC oxidation of primary alcohols genuinely stop at \text{R} - \text{CHO}
The incorrectly represented reaction is the \text{LiAlH}_4 one \rightarrow (A)

24948:
For a first order reaction k = \frac{0.693}{t_{1 / 2}}, so k_1(500 \text{ K}) = \frac{0.693}{2} = 0.3466 \text{ h}^{-1}
The Arrhenius relation gives \ln \frac{k(500)}{k(300)} = \frac{E_a}{R} \left(\frac{1}{300} - \frac{1}{500}\right) with \frac{1}{300} - \frac{1}{500} = \frac{1}{750}
For reaction 1, \ln 2 = \frac{E_{a1}}{R} \times \frac{1}{750}, so \frac{E_{a1}}{R} = 750 \ln 2
Reaction 2 has E_{a2} = E_{a1} / 2, hence \ln \frac{k_2(500)}{k_2(300)} = \frac{\ln 2}{2} = \ln \sqrt{2}
Thus k_2(300) = \frac{k_2(500)}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{2 \times 0.3466}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.49 = 4.9 \times 10^{-1}, nearest integer 5 → (5)

24953:
The series is geometric with first term (1 + x)^{1000} and ratio \frac{x}{1 + x} over 1001 terms
Sum S = (1 + x)^{1000} \times \frac{1 - \left(\frac{x}{1 + x}\right)^{1001}}{1 - \frac{x}{1 + x}}
The denominator is 1 - \frac{x}{1 + x} = \frac{1}{1 + x}
Hence S = (1 + x)^{1001} - x^{1001}
The coefficient of x^{499} in S is \binom{1001}{499} and of x^{500} is \binom{1001}{500}
Pascal's rule gives \binom{1001}{499} + \binom{1001}{500} = \binom{1002}{500}
This matches ^{1002}\text{C}_{500} → (D)
